1 | web-frontend | Branch feat/w1-ledger from origin/main. Read docs/proposals/fleet-optimization.md first; section 5 W1 is the requirement and section 3 is the baseline the ledger must be able to reproduce. STEP ONE, an inventory, written into docs/ledger.md before any code: for each provider launcher (first-party, kimi, grok) state from real log files on this machine exactly what a finished seat records (duration, API time, turns, input, output and cache tokens, cost, model), naming the file and field for each, and state plainly where a provider records no cost or no tokens; no field is assumed. STEP TWO, scripts/ledger.py (standard library only): builds logs/fleet-ledger.jsonl, one record per seat run, from the existing dispatch run logs, stream event files and plan headers, with repository, initiative (plan directory and the issue named in the header), plan, branch, PR when known (gh, optional, marked unverified when skipped), round (from the plan name or header), role, provider, model, tier (from a TIER header when present, else unknown), start, end, active time, waiting time between waves, outcome, tokens and cost where recorded, and cost_known false where not; idempotent, rebuilding from logs never duplicates a record. STEP THREE, rollups: per round, per PR, per initiative and per day, printed by make ledger (terminal, plain text, 100 columns) and written as ledger.json that the desk can read; the desk shows one line per initiative on the Floor under INITIATIVES with cost and elapsed and work share, and says cost unknown where it is. STEP FOUR, the orchestrator session line, per owner decision: make ledger-orchestrator DATE=YYYY-MM-DD USD=amount NOTE=text appends a manual daily reading marked source manual; the rollup shows it as its own line and never uses it to cap, warn about or throttle anything. STEP FIVE, prove it against the baseline: run the ledger over the 2026-09-13 and 2026-09-14 logs and paste the per-PR rollup for dev-agents PRs 79, 82 and 89 and the first-party cost total for 2026-09-13 (section 3 says about 178 dollars; state the ledger figure and the difference). Tests in tests/run-ledger-tests.sh with small fixtures copied from real logs: a first-party seat with cost, a kimi seat without cost, a grok seat without cost, a failed seat, a two-wave dispatch with waiting time, a rebuild that does not duplicate, a manual orchestrator reading. VERIFY: tests/run-ledger-tests.sh only, plus the make ledger output over the real logs pasted in the PR body; no full suite. Update section 8 of docs/proposals/fleet-optimization.md to record decisions 2 and 3 as taken, with the dates. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name in commit messages or PR text, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Open a draft PR titled Fleet optimization W1: the ledger. | feat/w1-ledger

2 | plan-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the PR on branch feat/w1-ledger. Tier C, round 1 of at most 2. No commits, no push, no review, no labels; tmp writes only. Record head SHA at start and at report. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: can the ledger tell a lie. Check: every field in docs/ledger.md is traced to a real log line; a seat with no recorded cost shows cost unknown, never zero; a rebuild never duplicates; waiting time is not counted as work; the 2026-09-13 first-party total matches an independent sum of total_cost_usd over the same logs; the orchestrator manual reading appears only as its own line and nothing reads it to restrict anything. Report only findings that make a number wrong or misleading. Post ONE comment on the PR whose first line reads CRITIC W1 LEDGER with SAFE-TO-MERGE, BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE and citations. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. | feat/w1-ledger
